### Response:
The given code has several flaws. It doesn't use prepared statements which are essential to prevent SQL injection attacks. Here's the correct way of doing it using PDO with Prepared Statements, ensuring that all data is properly escaped and validated before being used in a query:

```php
try {
    // Assuming $link is already established connection 
    $stmt = $link->prepare("SELECT * FROM users WHERE username= :username");
    $stmt->execute(['username' => $username]);
    $user = $stmt->fetch(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);

    if ($user) { // If there is a row...
        echo "User exists!";
    } else { 
        echo "No such user.";
    }
} catch (Exception $e) {
    die("Database error: " . $e->getMessage());
}
```
In the above code, we are using a prepared statement which means that an SQL command is sent to the database server only once and then it can be reused with different parameters. This prevents SQL injection as well because the parameters are never embedded directly in the query string but instead are sent as separate data items. The PHP PDO extension provides this functionality and handles all the necessary escaping for us, making our lives easier when working with databases.

Keep in mind that to use prepared statements we have a connection `$link` to a database established before. We assume here that you've already connected using appropriate credentials. If not, follow these steps: [https://www.php.net/manual/en/pdo.construct.php](https://www.php.net/manual/en/pdo.construct.php)
